#File and Folder Compression -- tar
tar => Tape Archive
A tool for bundling multiple files and folders into a single archive file (often called a "tarball"), and for extracting them later

1. Compress a folder (Create an Archive)
Bash
    ```tar -czvf <archive-name.tar.gz> <folder_to_compress>/

2. Decompress an archive (Extract everything)
Bash
    ```tar -xzvf <archive-name.tar.gz>

Breaking Down the Flags
    ** -c (Create): Tells tar to pack files together and make a brand-new archive.
    ** -x (eXtract): Tells tar to unwrap an existing archive.
    ** -z (Gzip): Compresses the archive using Gzip to make the file size much smaller. Without this flag, tar just clumps the files together into a large, uncompressed block.
    ** -v (Verbose): Optional but helpful. It lists every file in the terminal as it gets packed or unpacked so you can watch the progress live.
    ** -f (File): Tells tar that the very next word you type is going to be the filename of the archive. This flag must always go last right before the archive name.


Common Real-World Scenarios
1. Checking what is inside an archive without extracting it
Want to peek inside to see its contents before dumping hundreds of files into your working directory, use the -t (list) flag:
Bash
    ```tar -tzf archive-name.tar.gz

2. Extracting files to a specific directory
Unpack files into a specific folder, add the capital -C flag followed by the target path
Bash
    ```tar -xzvf archive-name.tar.gz -C /home/armstrong/Desktop/Copy/backup-new/

3. Excluding specific folders & files (like node_modules & .git)
Method 1: Repeat the --exclude Flag
Bash
    ```tar -xzvf archive-name.tar.gz -C /home/armstrong/Desktop/Copy/backup-new/
    ```tar --exclude='node_modules' --exclude='.git' --exclude='.env' -czvf project.tar.gz my-web-app/

Method 2: Use an Exclude File (Best for large projects)
1. Create your exclusion list file
Create a file named exclude-list.txt and list the items you want to skip, one per line:
Plaintext
    ```
        node_modules
        .git
        .env
        *.log
        dist
2. Run the tar command with -X
Bash
    ```tar -czvf project.tar.gz -X exclude-list.txt my-web-app/
tar will read exclude-list.txt first, map out everything it needs to skip, and then bundle up the rest of your app.

















